WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.707 --> 00:00:14.561
Imagine being separated from your family and friends for many years, even though they live just a few hundred meters away on the other side of a concrete wall.

2
00:00:14.562 --> 00:00:22.228
If you attempt to bypass the wall to see them, you will be severely punished or maybe killed by armed guards.

3
00:00:22.229 --> 00:00:32.906
This scenario may sound implausible, but this is the true story of the people affected by the Berlin Wall, a barrier that once divided East and West Berlin.

4
00:00:32.907 --> 00:00:35.549
At the End of World War II

5
00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:43.384
After World War II, Germany was split into four zones, which were each occupied by a different foreign army.

6
00:00:43.385 --> 00:00:53.045
The capitalist French, British, and Americans managed the western parts of the country, while the communist Soviets controlled the eastern section.

7
00:00:53.046 --> 00:01:00.864
Capitalists believe that individuals should have the right to make their own money in a market economy and keep what they earn.

8
00:01:00.865 --> 00:01:12.805
Communists, on the other hand, believe that wealth should be spread equally across a society, so the rich must give up some of their property to be redistributed to poorer families.

9
00:01:12.806 --> 00:01:23.744
The conflict between these two belief systems, and the nations that espoused them, became known as the Cold War (1947-1991).

10
00:01:23.745 --> 00:01:32.888
This struggle for dominance between the United States and the Soviet Union led to raised military and political tensions around the world.

11
00:01:32.889 --> 00:01:40.883
In Berlin, as in the rest of Germany, relations between communists and capitalists were particularly tense.

12
00:01:40.884 --> 00:01:55.714
Located deep inside East Germany, the capital had also been divided into two zones; West Berlin was in the hands of the allied British, French, and American governments, and the bigger East Berlin was controlled by the Soviets.

13
00:01:55.715 --> 00:02:06.758
Hostility between them grew during the 1950s, when millions of skilled workers left impoverished East Berlin to seek employment in the more prosperous West.

14
00:02:06.759 --> 00:02:15.959
At the same time, many West Berliners entered the communist zone to buy cheap groceries at stores subsidized by the Soviet government.

15
00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:23.172
The East was losing its labor force while shoppers were taking advantage of the communist area's lower prices.

16
00:02:23.173 --> 00:02:33.668
Facing total economic collapse, East German leader Walter Ulbricht proposed building a barrier to prevent people from crossing over from one side to the other.

17
00:02:33.669 --> 00:02:36.518
The Construction of the Berlin Wall

18
00:02:36.519 --> 00:02:41.510
Construction on the barrier began on August 13, 1961.

19
00:02:41.511 --> 00:02:45.840
Many people were suddenly separated from their families and friends.

20
00:02:45.841 --> 00:02:58.378
West Berliners who happened to be shopping in East Berlin that day were informed that they would not be able to return home without risking their lives; East Berlin workers were cut off from their jobs in the West.

21
00:02:58.379 --> 00:03:01.703
Berlin was now a physically divided city.

22
00:03:01.704 --> 00:03:11.797
At first the fence only consisted of barbed wire. It was 155 kilometers long and sat just inside Soviet territory.

23
00:03:11.798 --> 00:03:22.516
To deter people from trying to cross, armed East German troops stood at regular intervals and were ordered to shoot anyone who attempted to enter or to leave East Berlin.

24
00:03:22.517 --> 00:03:27.960
Then, in June 1962, construction on another fence was started.

25
00:03:27.961 --> 00:03:35.187
The second barrier ran parallel to the first, but left a wide-open space of 91 meters between them.

26
00:03:35.188 --> 00:03:44.736
This area was covered in gravel to make it easy for guards to spot footprints left by people trying to escape, and filled with landmines.

27
00:03:44.737 --> 00:03:52.220
Because so many people were killed trying to cross this alley into West Berlin, it became known as the death strip.

28
00:03:52.221 --> 00:03:59.148
The final two phases of the wall's construction began in 1965 and 1975.

29
00:03:59.149 --> 00:04:04.948
The East German troops replaced the barbed wire fence with a solid concrete wall.

30
00:04:04.949 --> 00:04:15.139
Then they fortified it; in the end it had 45,000 different sections, each 3.6 meters high and 1.2 meters wide.

31
00:04:15.140 --> 00:04:22.870
There were also 116 watchtowers, 20 bunkers, and only 8 gates, each with armed guards.

32
00:04:22.871 --> 00:04:25.021
Famous Escape Attempts

33
00:04:25.022 --> 00:04:32.578
Despite such great obstacles, more than five thousand people did manage to flee from East Berlin to West Berlin.

34
00:04:32.579 --> 00:04:41.248
The first escapees were able to jump over the barbed wire fence through an apartment window or from the roof of a nearby building.

35
00:04:41.249 --> 00:04:52.116
In 1964 West Berliners were able to help 57 people escape through a 145-meter-long tunnel dug under the wire fence.

36
00:04:52.117 --> 00:04:59.386
After the completion of the concrete wall, however, defectors had to become more creative in their escape plans.

37
00:04:59.387 --> 00:05:06.199
Some were able to escape by sliding along a cable suspended over the fence between two buildings.

38
00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:15.112
One man was able to drive a low sports car under the barricade into freedom at Checkpoint Charlie, the main crossing point.

39
00:05:15.113 --> 00:05:27.145
Perhaps the most famous story of a successful escape is that of Thomas Kruger, who piloted a small aircraft, stolen from an East German military training camp, into West Berlin.

40
00:05:27.146 --> 00:05:36.019
After landing safely he dismantled the plane and sent it in pieces to the East Germans with written messages such as "Wish you were here."

41
00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:42.535
Unfortunately, not everyone who tried to escape East Germany made it across the border alive.

42
00:05:42.536 --> 00:05:47.175
As many as 239 people were killed trying to cross.

43
00:05:47.176 --> 00:05:56.044
The most famous case happened in 1962, when a young man named Peter Fletcher was shot as he was scaling the outer wall.

44
00:05:56.045 --> 00:06:07.147
He fell into the death strip, and even though he was still alive, neither onlookers nor the media could offer him assistance, as East German guards would certainly have shot them.

45
00:06:07.148 --> 00:06:10.711
Fletcher's death was captured on film by the media.

46
00:06:10.712 --> 00:06:17.716
As the images of this horrible event circulated around the world, they became a symbol of communist tyranny.

47
00:06:17.717 --> 00:06:20.041
The Fall of the Berlin Wall

48
00:06:20.042 --> 00:06:29.207
Almost forty years after the official separation of East and West Berlin, the prospect of a unified Germany seemed as out of reach as ever.

49
00:06:29.208 --> 00:06:33.310
But several unexpected events occurred in 1989.

50
00:06:33.311 --> 00:06:44.729
In August of that year, communist Hungary and capitalist Austria opened their borders to each other, allowing thirteen thousand East Germans to flee via this new route.

51
00:06:44.730 --> 00:06:53.286
Huge demonstrations began to take place all over East Germany, which caused the leader of East Germany, Erich Honecker, to resign.

52
00:06:53.287 --> 00:07:03.503
The new East German leader, Egon Krenz, had decided to draft a policy that would allow East Berliners to apply for visas to travel to West Berlin.

53
00:07:03.504 --> 00:07:11.074
On November 9 the Minister of Propaganda, Gunter Schabowski, was scheduled to meet with the press.

54
00:07:11.075 --> 00:07:17.997
He was informed of the proposed changes, but was still unaware that the policy had not yet been finalized.

55
00:07:17.998 --> 00:07:24.425
He announced on the air that all East Berliners would be allowed to cross the border with proper permission.

56
00:07:24.426 --> 00:07:30.558
When asked how soon this policy would take effect, he responded that it was effective immediately.

57
00:07:30.559 --> 00:07:40.376
Tens of thousands of East Berliners who had listened to the minister's statement on television headed to the checkpoints, demanding that they be allowed to cross.

58
00:07:40.377 --> 00:07:45.591
The guards had no idea how to handle such crowds and could not control them.

59
00:07:45.592 --> 00:07:53.525
As thousands of East Berliners stampeded across the barrier, they were met by anxious families and old friends living in the West.

60
00:07:53.526 --> 00:08:00.079
Soon excited Germans were destroying the wall with sledgehammers and even their bare hands.

61
00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:07.181
The symbol of a divided country was being destroyed, and citizens rejoiced at the end of the painful era.

62
00:08:07.182 --> 00:08:16.333
Today little of the Berlin Wall remains standing, but a few sections have been left to remind Germans of this sad episode in their history.

63
00:08:16.334 --> 00:08:24.153
Germany has since reunited under a single democratic leadership, but the effects of the separation are long lasting.

64
00:08:24.154 --> 00:08:30.881
Many talk about the many differences in culture that emerged between the East and West during their time apart.

65
00:08:30.882 --> 00:08:39.850
But most are just happy that they have been reunited with their families and friends, and together they look toward a future as one nation.
